course: space
lesson: 1
title: The Sun, our star
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Sun is a star, the same kind of thing as the tiny dots you see at night, only so close that it fills our day with light.
explain: It is a gigantic ball of hot gas: if the Sun were a football, the Earth would be a peppercorn about 25 metres away.
explain: The Sun does not burn like a bonfire, there is no oxygen fire at all; deep in its core hydrogen is squeezed into helium, and that nuclear fusion gives out the light and heat.
explain: Light is fast but the Sun is far, so when you watch a sunset you see the Sun as it was about 8 minutes earlier.
explain: The Sun is not calm: it has dark sunspots, flares and a wind of particles that, when it hits the Earth's magnetic field, lights up the northern lights.
explain: Never look straight at the Sun, not even for a moment and not through binoculars, because it can blind you in seconds; sunspots are safe to see only through proper solar filters or by projecting the Sun's picture onto white card.
fact: The Sun is a star, an ordinary yellow dwarf, and it holds about 99.8 percent of all the mass in the Solar System.
fact: About 109 Earths would fit side by side across the Sun, and about 1.3 million Earths would fit inside it.
fact: The Earth is about 150 million kilometres from the Sun, and sunlight takes about 8 minutes 20 seconds to reach us.
fact: In the Sun's core, hydrogen nuclei are squeezed together to make helium, and this nuclear fusion releases the energy that makes the Sun shine.
fact: The Sun is not a fire: nothing burns with oxygen there.
fact: The Sun's visible surface, the photosphere, is about 5,500 degrees Celsius, but its thin outer atmosphere, the corona, is over a million degrees; exactly why the corona is so hot is still being studied.
fact: Sunspots are darker, cooler patches where strong magnetic fields stop hot gas rising; they look black only because the rest of the Sun is brighter, and many are bigger than the Earth.
fact: The number of sunspots rises and falls in a cycle of about 11 years, and the most recent peak, solar maximum, was reported to have come around 2024 to 2025.
fact: The Sun constantly blows out charged particles called the solar wind, which flows past all the planets at hundreds of kilometres per second.
fact: The Earth's magnetic field shields us from most of the solar wind, and particles leaking in near the poles make the aurora, the northern and southern lights; near solar maximum, as in 2024, aurorae were seen much further from the poles than usual, even in England.
fact: Solar flares and huge clouds of gas called coronal mass ejections can disturb satellites, radio and even power grids on Earth.
fact: The Sun is about 4.6 billion years old, roughly halfway through its life; in about 5 billion years it will swell into a red giant and end as a white dwarf.
fact: The Sun is a ball of gas, so different parts spin at different speeds: about 25 days at its equator and about 35 days near its poles; astronomers first noticed the Sun spins by watching sunspots move across its face.
exercise: What is the Sun? | A star, an ordinary yellow dwarf | A planet that glows because it is hot | A huge fire burning wood and coal | A ball of burning oxygen gas
exercise: How much of all the mass in the Solar System is in the Sun? | About 99.8 percent | About half of all of it | About 10 percent of it | About three quarters of it
exercise: About how many Earths would fit side by side across the Sun? | About 109 | About 10 | About 1,000 | About 3
exercise: About how many Earths would fit inside the Sun? | About 1.3 million | About 109 | About 1,000 | About 1 billion of them
exercise: How far is the Earth from the Sun? | About 150 million kilometres | About 150 thousand kilometres | About 384,000 kilometres | About 15 billion kilometres
exercise: How long does sunlight take to reach the Earth? | About 8 minutes 20 seconds | It arrives at the very same instant | About one second | About one day
exercise: What makes the Sun shine? | Hydrogen squeezed into helium in its core, nuclear fusion | Coal and gas burning with oxygen | Light reflected from the other stars | Lightning storms on its surface
exercise: How hot is the Sun's visible surface, the photosphere? | About 5,500 degrees Celsius | About 100 degrees Celsius | About 1 million degrees Celsius | About 55 degrees Celsius
exercise: Which part of the Sun is over a million degrees? | The corona, its thin outer atmosphere | The photosphere, its visible surface | The sunspots on its face | No part; the whole Sun is cooler than that
exercise: What are sunspots? | Darker, cooler patches held down by strong magnetic fields | Holes that go right through the Sun | Shadows of planets passing in front | Patches where the Sun has burnt out
exercise: How long is the sunspot cycle? | About 11 years | About 1 year | About 100 years | About 27 days
exercise: When did the most recent solar maximum come? | Around 2024 to 2025 | Around 1990 | It has not happened for centuries | Around 2050
exercise: What is the solar wind? | A stream of charged particles blowing out from the Sun | Air that blows across space all the way from the Sun to the Earth | Warm breezes made by sunshine on Earth | Gas sucked into the Sun from space
exercise: What can a big solar flare or coronal mass ejection disturb? | Satellites, radio and power grids | The length of the year and the seasons | The Moon's path in its orbit round us | The colour of the sea and of the sky
exercise: How old is the Sun? | About 4.6 billion years | About 6,000 years | About 100 million years | About 46 billion years
exercise: What will the Sun become in about 5 billion years? | A red giant | A black hole | A planet | A neutron star
exercise: Why does the Sun spin faster at its equator than near its poles? | It is a ball of gas, so its parts can spin at different speeds | It is solid rock that has cracked in two | The planets push its middle round faster | The solar wind pushes its poles backwards
test: my mate says the Sun's just a massive bonfire up there. right? | No, it shines by nuclear fusion, nothing burns with oxygen | Yes, it burns just like wood on a fire, only very much bigger | Yes, and it will burn out next century | No, it is a mirror reflecting other stars
test: If the Sun suddenly switched off, how long would it be before we noticed the dark? | About 8 minutes, the time light takes to reach us | At that very same instant, because light is instant | About a year later, when the Earth goes round once | Never, because the Moon would keep us lit up instead
test: If the Sun is just an ordinary star, why does it look so much bigger and brighter than the others? | It is very close; the other stars are suns much further away | It really is the biggest star in the universe | The other stars are only small lamps in the sky | Stars only shine at night when the Sun is off
test: Why do sunspots look black if they are still thousands of degrees hot? | The rest of the Sun around them is even brighter | They are cold, dead parts of the Sun | They are holes showing empty space behind | They are covered in thick black smoke and ash from fires
test: Lily wants to look at sunspots through Dad's binoculars. What should she do? | Not do it; look only with proper solar filters or projection | Do it, but only for a few seconds | Do it with sunglasses on | Do it at midday, when the Sun is clearest and the spots are biggest
test: Why might a big solar storm worry an electricity company? | It can disturb power grids and knock out supply | It can make the Sun go dark for a whole week or more | It can heat the cables in the ground until they melt | It can stop the Earth from turning for a few hours
test: In a cartoon, the Sun is a cheerful planet with a face. What is wrong? | The Sun is a star, not a planet | The Sun is really a moon | The Sun is actually smaller than the Earth | Nothing, the Sun is the biggest planet
test: My granddad says he saw the northern lights from England in 2024. Could that be true? | Yes, near solar maximum aurorae were seen much further south than usual | No, aurorae happen only at the North Pole | No, aurorae are just a story from films | Yes, but only because it was winter
test: Which is surprising about the Sun's temperatures? | The thin outer corona is far hotter than the visible surface | The surface is hotter than the core | The Sun is coldest at its centre | Sunspots are the hottest part of the Sun
test: How did astronomers first notice that the Sun spins? | By watching sunspots move across its face | By hearing it whirr and hum through a big telescope | By seeing its rings go round | By noticing the year getting shorter
test: ugh the Sun's gonna explode tomorrow, saw it on TikTok lol | No, it is halfway through its life and will not explode; it will become a red giant in billions of years | Yes, scientists say it will blow up any day | Yes, it explodes every 11 years | No, it will just switch off next year
exam: Identify the process by which energy is generated within the core of the Sun. | Nuclear fusion of hydrogen into helium | Combustion of gases in the presence of oxygen | Reflection of light received from other stars | Gravitational friction between the planets
exam: Ascertain the approximate interval required for light emitted by the Sun to reach the Earth. | Approximately eight minutes and twenty seconds | Approximately one second, about as long as a blink | Approximately one hour, the time of a short journey | It is received at once, without any delay whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises sunspots? | Comparatively cool regions in which strong magnetic fields inhibit rising gas | Apertures through which the interior of the Sun may be observed | Shadows cast upon the Sun by the passage of the planets | Regions in which fusion has permanently ceased
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the future evolution of the Sun. | In some five billion years it shall expand into a red giant | It shall collapse into a black hole within the present century | It shall explode as a supernova within a thousand years | It shall remain wholly unchanged in perpetuity
